    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "got it. so basically, if we don't do this, we just end up with a bunch of messy, duplicate data scattered everywhere. what are the biggest risks if we just leave it in 2nf instead of taking the time to normalize it all the way?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The biggest risk is incurring update anomalies; if a city changes its associated zip code, you would have to update that information in every single row for every order in that city. Deletion anomalies occur when deleting an order inadvertently removes necessary information, like the municipal details for a city that still exists. Furthermore, insertion anomalies prevent you from adding new data—like a new city and its zip code—unless an actual order is placed within that city.",
      "source": "target",
